<body>
    <h1>Training Object Detection</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p><strong>Object detection</strong> is a computer vision technolog=
y that enables your applications not only to process an image or video but =
also to identify and locate specific items within it. The idea is to give a=
n app the functionality to distinguish between different types of fruits in=
 a photo, identify furniture in a room through the camera, or even count sp=
ecific objects in a scene. There are countless possibilities, and they can =
add tremendous value to the user experience.</p>
<p>Traditionally, training machine learning models for this task could be a=
n intimidating process, requiring specialized knowledge and complex tools. =
However, for developers in the Apple ecosystem, <strong>CreateML</strong> i=
s a powerful and accessible solution that greatly simplifies the complexity=
 of building your own model.</p>
<p>Let=E2=80=99s explore how to train an object detector using CreateML, fr=
om preparing and annotating your image data to obtaining a functional <code=
>.mlmodel</code> ready to be integrated into an app.</p>
<h3 id=3D"TrainingObjectDetection-WhyUseanObjectDetector?">Why Use an Objec=
t Detector?</h3>
<p>Integrating an object detector into your app lets you implement innovati=
ve features that rely on understanding visual content. When the app is able=
 to "know" which objects are present in an image or camera feed and where t=
hey're located, you can build smarter, more interactive, and more contextua=
l experiences for users. Some common use cases include:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Mixed Reality and Sports:</strong> With platforms like Apple Vis=
ion Pro, it=E2=80=99s possible to create entirely new sports viewing experi=
ences, such as projecting an NBA court onto a tabletop in your environment.=
 For the virtual court to be realistically and stably positioned, the syste=
m needs to identify suitable real-world surfaces. A trained object detector=
 that recognizes tables (or other flat surfaces) is essential to enable thi=
s functionality, anchoring digital content to the user=E2=80=99s physical s=
pace.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Augmented Reality (AR):</strong> Object detection is fundamental=
 for more immersive AR experiences. It allows virtual objects to interact m=
ore realistically with the physical environment, such as placing a virtual =
vase on a real table detected by the camera.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Photography:</strong> Camera apps can use detection to automatic=
ally optimize settings (e.g., portrait mode when a person is detected) or h=
elp frame the photo better based on the objects of interest.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"TrainingObjectDetection-GettingStarted">Getting Started</h3>
<p>The first step is to <strong>clearly define which object or objects</str=
ong> you want your model to learn to detect. With that in mind, the main ta=
sk is to <strong>build your dataset</strong>.</p>
<p>Creating datasets for computer vision tasks like object detection usuall=
y requires some effort. The reason is that it=E2=80=99s not enough to have =
just the images; you also need <strong>annotations</strong>=E2=80=94precise=
 information that indicates <em>where</em> each object of interest is locat=
ed within each image.</p>
<p>In other words, for CreateML to train a model, you need to provide "solv=
ed" examples. This involves manual work (or the use of specific tools) to d=
raw <strong>bounding boxes</strong> around objects in each image and record=
 this information, usually in a JSON file. Essentially, you=E2=80=99re manu=
ally performing the task you want the model to learn to do automatically.</=
p>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>At this stage, you might find a dataset that already includes annotation=
s. There are good sources that can be helpful when you're just getting star=
ted.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"TrainingObjectDetection-What=E2=80=99sanAnnotation?">What=E2=80=
=99s an Annotation?</h3>
<p>Simply put, an <strong>annotation</strong> in the context of object dete=
ction is the structured information that describes <strong>which object</st=
rong> is present in an image and <strong>exactly where</strong> it is locat=
ed. It usually includes a <strong>label</strong> for the object (e.g., "dog=
", "car", "table") and the <strong>coordinates of the bounding box</strong>=
 that encloses it.</p>
<p>There are different formats (standards) for structuring these annotation=
s. CreateML expects a specific JSON-based format. We=E2=80=99ll use Apple=
=E2=80=99s recommended standard, which structures the information as follow=
s:</p>
<p><strong>.JSON File Structure</strong></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
<table data-table-width=3D"760" data-layout=3D"default" data-local-id=3D"4e=
c72ce8-59b1-4881-9ba6-cb3902d23950" class=3D"confluenceTable">
<tbody>
<tr>
<th class=3D"confluenceTh">
<p>Name</p></th>
<th class=3D"confluenceTh">
<p>Type</p></th>
<th class=3D"confluenceTh">
<p>Description</p></th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p><code>imagefilename</code></p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>String</p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>The name of the image file.</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p><code>annotation</code></p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>Array</p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>An array (list) of annotation JSON objects.</p></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Each JSON object in the <code>annotation</code> array should follow this=
 structure:</p>
<p><strong>Annotation Object Structure (inside the annotation array)</stron=
g></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
<table data-table-width=3D"760" data-layout=3D"default" data-local-id=3D"41=
fdcb53-d750-4f7c-9f93-ab1e1e8bfdbe" class=3D"confluenceTable">
<tbody>
<tr>
<th class=3D"confluenceTh">
<p>Name</p></th>
<th class=3D"confluenceTh">
<p>Type</p></th>
<th class=3D"confluenceTh">
<p>Description</p></th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p><code>label</code></p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>String</p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>The name (label) of the annotated object.</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p><code>coordinates</code></p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>JSON Object</p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>The location and area the object occupies in the image.</p></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class=3D"confluence-information-macro confluence-information-macro-not=
e">
<span class=3D"aui-icon aui-icon-small aui-iconfont-warning confluence-info=
rmation-macro-icon"></span>
<div class=3D"confluence-information-macro-body">
<p>In our case, instead of using <em>imagefilename</em>, we completed the w=
hole process using just the <em>image</em> label.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>The <code>coordinates</code> JSON object must have the following structu=
re:</p>
<p><code>coordinates</code><strong> Structure Object</strong></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
<table data-table-width=3D"760" data-layout=3D"default" data-local-id=3D"55=
ee4ca5-648b-408c-ac0a-01d07e460083" class=3D"confluenceTable">
<tbody>
<tr>
<th class=3D"confluenceTh">
<p>Name</p></th>
<th class=3D"confluenceTh">
<p>Type</p></th>
<th class=3D"confluenceTh">
<p>Description</p></th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p><code>x</code></p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>Number</p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>The x-coordinate of the annotation anchor point relative to the image or=
igin, defined by <code>MLBoundingBoxCoordinatesOrigin</code>.</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p><code>y</code></p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>Number</p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>The y-coordinate of the annotation anchor point relative to the image or=
igin, defined by <code>MLBoundingBoxCoordinatesOrigin</code>.</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p><code>width</code></p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>Number</p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>The width of the annotation=E2=80=99s bounding box.</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p><code>height</code></p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>Number</p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>The height of the annotation=E2=80=99s bounding box.</p></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>In our example, we=E2=80=99re going to build a small dataset and train a=
 model to recognize <strong>dogs</strong> in images.</p>
<h2 id=3D"TrainingObjectDetection-PreparingtheDataset">Preparing the Datase=
t</h2>
<p>Let=E2=80=99s now download the images we=E2=80=99ll use to train our mod=
el and then create annotations for each of them.</p>
<p>When downloading images, it=E2=80=99s important to consider that using v=
ery large and heavy files can make the training process unnecessarily long.=
 Ideally, you should adapt to the context: in most cases, ultra-high-resolu=
tion images are not needed, but there may be situations where specific deta=
ils in the image are crucial and justify higher resolution.</p><span class=
=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedd=
ed-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=
=3D"training.png" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"6e9459f027f679e5d4d=
4d542f40e3b1e8dda569f93349ee4e20b5fbbc120011f" data-image-src=3D"https://de=
veloperacademy.atlassian.net/wiki/download/attachments/1208877272/training.=
png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1750692278148&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&a=
mp;api=3Dv2" data-height=3D"426" data-width=3D"854" data-unresolved-comment=
-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1208877305" data-linked-resource-ve=
rsion=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-d=
efault-alias=3D"training.png" data-base-url=3D"https://developeracademy.atl=
assian.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linke=
d-resource-container-id=3D"1208877272" data-linked-resource-container-versi=
on=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"2579a91e-04eb-4b78-812e-5daa8ebba9ef" data-media-=
type=3D"file" height=3D"233"></span>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>Remember that a good practice in dataset preparation is to divide it int=
o Training, Testing, and optionally Validation sets. Annotations should be =
created for all images used in each set.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"TrainingObjectDetection-CreatingourAnnotations">Creating our Anno=
tations</h3>
<p>Now, for each image in our dataset, we=E2=80=99ll create an annotation f=
ollowing the JSON format expected by CreateML, as in the example below, whi=
ch specifies the object=E2=80=99s <code>label</code> and its <code>coordina=
tes</code> in the image:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">[
  {
    "annotations": [
      {
        "coordinates": {
          "height": 344,
          "width": 278,
          "x": 224,
          "y": 206
        },
        "label": "dog"
      }
    ],
    "image": "n02085620_11258.jpg"
  },
  {
    "annotations": [
      {
        "coordinates": {
=09 // ... more images
 ]
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>There are several graphical tools that can help us visually create these=
 annotations, many of which are available on the macOS App Store or online.=
 These tools let you =E2=80=9Cdraw=E2=80=9D the bounding boxes over the obj=
ects and export the JSON in the required format.</p>
<p>Here=E2=80=99s an example of how a tool can simplify the annotation proc=
ess:</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrappe=
r confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image =
image-center" alt=3D"examp.jpg" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"1907d=
18cae4eebf3fe99f6cc82ee9f7ce3ccb92e272eb9845a5f1e843759c095" data-image-src=
=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wiki/download/attachments/120887=
7272/examp.jpg?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1750692278147&amp;cacheVe=
rsion=3D1&amp;api=3Dv2" data-height=3D"1327" data-width=3D"3013" data-unres=
olved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1208877302" data-linke=
d-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-link=
ed-resource-default-alias=3D"examp.jpg" data-base-url=3D"https://developera=
cademy.atlassian.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/jpeg"=
 data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"1208877272" data-linked-resource-cont=
ainer-version=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"f3b8a66b-3034-4dc9-9bd2-b8c525c62ae1" =
data-media-type=3D"file" height=3D"205"></span>
<p>As a result, the software usually generates a JSON file with the corresp=
onding annotations you made for each image.</p>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>Be sure to check the JSON format that the tool you choose produces. In s=
ome cases, the structure may differ from what CreateML expects, and you mig=
ht need to adjust the JSON file before using it for training.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>With your images collected and annotations ready, you can move on to tra=
ining the model.</p>
<h2 id=3D"TrainingObjectDetection-Trainingourmodel">Training our model</h2>
<p>To train the model, we=E2=80=99ll use <strong>CreateML</strong>, Apple=
=E2=80=99s tool designed to simplify this process.</p>
<p>Just launch CreateML, select <strong>"New Document"</strong> and choose =
the <strong>Object Detection</strong> template.</p><span class=3D"confluenc=
e-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-siz=
e"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=3D"t1.png" wid=
th=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"5b2e4c74e43532e9f51a9ce26ef10214787ef572=
caa2a148072b7df2bdd64872" data-image-src=3D"https://developeracademy.atlass=
ian.net/wiki/download/attachments/1208877272/t1.png?version=3D1&amp;modific=
ationDate=3D1750692277921&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&amp;api=3Dv2" data-height=3D=
"888" data-width=3D"1162" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-r=
esource-id=3D"1208877299" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-re=
source-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"t1.png" da=
ta-base-url=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wiki" data-linked-res=
ource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"12088=
77272" data-linked-resource-container-version=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"0f2ca4=
a6-a299-4810-8fda-55ed7e00ec2b" data-media-type=3D"file" height=3D"357"></s=
pan><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper co=
nfluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image imag=
e-center" alt=3D"t2.png" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"247ce05ecabc=
ce33743ba37fa3c2ba4660ec7506794a0fecdc585bfaa11ff2a8" data-image-src=3D"htt=
ps://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wiki/download/attachments/1208877272/t2=
.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1750692277920&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&=
amp;api=3Dv2" data-height=3D"884" data-width=3D"1512" data-unresolved-comme=
nt-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1208877293" data-linked-resource-=
version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource=
-default-alias=3D"t2.png" data-base-url=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassi=
an.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-re=
source-container-id=3D"1208877272" data-linked-resource-container-version=
=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"ebaa9c66-2be6-4a83-849e-47a9bf9d6418" data-media-ty=
pe=3D"file" height=3D"273"></span>
<p>Next, we select the folders containing the images and annotations for th=
e Training, Validation, and Testing sets.</p><span class=3D"confluence-embe=
dded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><im=
g class=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=3D"t3.png" width=3D"=
468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"8817342b12d252b84254029149eee2d3600df1a07950c8=
bd5b273dd507fcbd5b" data-image-src=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassian.ne=
t/wiki/download/attachments/1208877272/t3.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationD=
ate=3D1750692277921&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&amp;api=3Dv2" data-height=3D"884" =
data-width=3D"1512" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resourc=
e-id=3D"1208877296" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource=
-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"t3.png" data-bas=
e-url=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-=
content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"1208877272"=
 data-linked-resource-container-version=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"0fa33afc-a52=
a-40b0-b184-b39a9d01205f" data-media-type=3D"file" height=3D"273"></span>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>In our example, we didn=E2=80=99t provide a separate validation set. Cre=
ateML automatically reserves a portion of the training set for validation d=
uring the process.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>In the Training Parameters configuration, after some experimentation for=
 this example, we achieved good results with the following settings:</p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
<table data-table-width=3D"760" data-layout=3D"default" data-local-id=3D"f8=
123670-7a78-4520-918b-9cd51678d24a" class=3D"confluenceTable">
<tbody>
<tr>
<th class=3D"confluenceTh">
<p>Parameter</p></th>
<th class=3D"confluenceTh">
<p>Selected</p></th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p><code>Algorithm</code></p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>Transfer Learning</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p><code>Iterations</code></p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>100</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p><code>Batch Size</code></p></td>
<td class=3D"confluenceTd">
<p>128</p></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>It=E2=80=99s important to note that these parameters may not be ideal fo=
r <em>every</em> dataset. It=E2=80=99s worth testing different configuratio=
ns (especially the number of <code>Iterations</code>) to find what works be=
st for your specific case.</p>
<p>When you click the <strong>"Train"</strong> button, the training process=
 begins, and you can monitor the progress through the graphical interface:<=
/p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper con=
fluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image image=
-center" alt=3D"t4.png" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"9aff7ed1da4dc=
c961df66e4039f35c71108b7609afcfe61df044f8eed8b6e00f" data-image-src=3D"http=
s://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wiki/download/attachments/1208877272/t4.=
png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1750692277675&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&a=
mp;api=3Dv2" data-height=3D"884" data-width=3D"1512" data-unresolved-commen=
t-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1208877290" data-linked-resource-v=
ersion=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-=
default-alias=3D"t4.png" data-base-url=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassia=
n.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-res=
ource-container-id=3D"1208877272" data-linked-resource-container-version=3D=
"1" data-media-id=3D"d5579466-9760-467b-ae86-d1c1c503a25a" data-media-type=
=3D"file" height=3D"273"></span>
<p>After training, the <strong>"Evaluation"</strong> tab presents a summary=
 of the model=E2=80=99s performance:</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-=
file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img cla=
ss=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=3D"t5.png" width=3D"468" =
loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"e6dc71be928ccce3a1dfbd59a4ed3e44b6fb1f741c3d4cb5c84=
a1280116ec4f6" data-image-src=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wik=
i/download/attachments/1208877272/t5.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=
=3D1750692277675&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&amp;api=3Dv2" data-height=3D"765" dat=
a-width=3D"611" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=
=3D"1208877287" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-typ=
e=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"t5.png" data-base-ur=
l=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-cont=
ent-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"1208877272" dat=
a-linked-resource-container-version=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"13c96a01-3c07-41=
8d-9dca-8fcff2ec0542" data-media-type=3D"file" height=3D"585"></span>
<p>In this tab, you can review metrics such as Precision and Recall, as wel=
l as view examples of correct and incorrect detections (false positives and=
 false negatives), which helps to understand where the model is performing =
well or struggling.</p>
<h3 id=3D"TrainingObjectDetection-Exportingourmodel">Exporting our model</h=
3>
<p>To export our model as a Core ML model file, go to the <strong>Output</s=
trong> tab and select one of the available options: <strong>Get</strong> or=
 <strong>Xcode</strong>. You can export either the final result of your mod=
el or a snapshot taken during the training process.</p><span class=3D"confl=
uence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual=
-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=3D"t6.png"=
 width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"47370a4e9888e4931f8d30c687e8b3f125ff=
c5af80c36208ddab513607225f3b" data-image-src=3D"https://developeracademy.at=
lassian.net/wiki/download/attachments/1208877272/t6.png?version=3D1&amp;mod=
ificationDate=3D1750692277674&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&amp;api=3Dv2" data-heigh=
t=3D"316" data-width=3D"1512" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-link=
ed-resource-id=3D"1208877284" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linke=
d-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"t6.png=
" data-base-url=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wiki" data-linked=
-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"1=
208877272" data-linked-resource-container-version=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"2c=
7440f8-f9e7-4ec6-a863-9be18b2ce690" data-media-type=3D"file" height=3D"97">=
</span>
<h3 id=3D"TrainingObjectDetection-IntegratingtheObjectDetectorintoYourApp">=
Integrating the Object Detector into Your App</h3>
<p>Once your object detector is finished, export it as a Core ML <code>.mlm=
odel</code> file and add it to your Xcode project:</p>
<p>Here=E2=80=99s an example of how to integrate your model using the Visio=
n framework:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">import V=
ision
import CoreML

let model =3D try VNCoreMLModel(for: MyObjectDetector_Dogs_Tutorial_1().mod=
el)

let request =3D VNCoreMLRequest(model: model) { request, error in
    guard let results =3D request.results as? [VNClassificationObservation]=
,
          let topResult =3D results.first else { return }

    print("Action detected: \\(topResult.identifier) with confidence \\(top=
Result.confidence)")
}
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>Since this article is fully focused on training, we won=E2=80=99t go int=
o detail about how to deploy the trained model.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"TrainingObjectDetection-Conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
<p>This article showed how to use CreateML to train object detection models=
, an essential step for many modern iOS apps. We covered the importance of =
JSON annotations and how the resulting <code>.mlmodel</code> file can be us=
ed with the Core ML and Vision frameworks to analyze images.</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
